<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#standard-chinese">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Standard Chinese</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Standard Mandarin, Putonghua</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Standard Chinese, or Standard Mandarin, is the official language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, based on the pronunciation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and the grammar of modern vernacular Mandarin literature. Promoted as Putonghua ('common speech') from 1956, it is also an official language in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and is the most widely spoken form of Chinese.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/standard-chinese.jpg" alt="Standard Chinese" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kwamikagami · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mandarin_sphere.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
